        "h_title": "Goong pad sator - Bitter beans stir-fried with shrimp paste and prawns",
        "main_title": "Goong pad sator - Bitter beans stir-fried with shrimp paste and prawns",
        "author": "John Chantarasak",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 1,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "This speedy recipe for goong pad sator, or bitter beans stir fried with shrimp paste and prawns from John Chantarasak is a delicate balance of hot, sour, salt and sweet flavours. Serve with plenty of steamed white rice.",
            "url": "https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/collections/beans-recipes",
            "urls": [
              {
                "url": "https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/collections/beans-recipes",
                "anchor_text": "beans"
              },
              {
                "url": "https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/collections/shrimp-recipes",
                "anchor_text": "shrimp"
              },
              {
                "url": "https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/collections/prawn-recipes",
                "anchor_text": "prawns"
              },
              {
                "url": "https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/collections/rice-recipes",
                "anchor_text": "rice"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Sator or stink beans are ubiquitous in Southern Thailand and can be found in a number of the specialty dishes of the region. They are similar in appearance to a broad bean or edamame, and have a powerful flavour that’s pungent and bitter. Because of this, sator beans can be paired alongside other strong flavours like chilli, garlic and shrimp paste. Sator beans can be found in Asian supermarkets, but if you are unable to find them you could use fresh broad beans or edamame beans instead.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
